\begin_layout Section
Types and structures
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In what follows,
 
\begin_inset listings
lstparams "language=Python,fontfamily=tt,fontsize={\scriptsize},tabsize=4"
inline true
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
python
\end_layout

\end_inset

 serves as the display language wherever a construction is stated more clearly as code than as prose.
 Nothing below depends on that choice:
 every fragment shown is a transcription of a definition given in the text,
 and it is the definition that carries the argument.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The correspondence between mathematics and types is easiest to state if three questions are kept apart:
 which objects there are,
 which operations act on them,
 and which constants they distinguish.
 A structure is then a choice of all three at once,
 together with the laws binding them,
 and the four tables below follow exactly that division.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Objects
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Classical mathematics,
 and set theory in particular,
 keeps a container and its contents apart:
 a set on one side,
 its elements on the other.
 The separation does not survive the addition of structure.
 In topology,
 in order theory,
 in algebra,
 it is the elements that acquire the properties —
 they are added,
 compared,
 inverted,
 taken limits of —
 and the set is characterised by what its elements admit rather than by which of them happen to lie inside it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Type theory takes that as its starting point.
 A type is a collection of admissible values together with the operations those values admit;
 a value is an inhabitant of a type carrying a state,
 which is what mathematics would call a representation.
 Object-oriented programming reads the same way,
 and adds the observation that closes the picture:
 since a set may itself be an element of another set,
 a container is a value too,
 and therefore a type.
 Scalars,
 tuples,
 sets and maps are then all types,
 distinguished only by the operations they carry and not by their standing as container or content;
 the correspondence for the constructions used below is collected in 
