\begin_layout Subsection
Operations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Python names its operations in the data model:
 a fixed vocabulary of special methods,
 written with leading and trailing underscores,
 each bound to a piece of surface syntax.
 Implementing the method is what makes the syntax available on a new carrier,
 so a type declares its operations by supplying names from that vocabulary and by nothing else.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "tab:operators"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 is the resulting dictionary.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The dictionary is one-to-one on spelling and silent on meaning.
 Python fixes how an operation is written;
 the carrier fixes what it does.
 The symbol 
\begin_inset listings
lstparams "language=Python,fontfamily=tt,fontsize={\scriptsize},tabsize=4"
inline true
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
|
\end_layout

\end_inset

 is set union on a 
\begin_inset listings
lstparams "language=Python,fontfamily=tt,fontsize={\scriptsize},tabsize=4"
inline true
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
set
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 bitwise disjunction on an 
\begin_inset listings
lstparams "language=Python,fontfamily=tt,fontsize={\scriptsize},tabsize=4"
inline true
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
int
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 and a lattice join on the carriers built below,
 and nothing in the syntax chooses between them —
 which is precisely why the same operator may be reused rather than a new notation invented.
 The reverse translation is equally free:
 mathematics writes 
\begin_inset Formula $\wedge$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\vee$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\lnot$
\end_inset

 where the keyboard offers 
\begin_inset listings
lstparams "language=Python,fontfamily=tt,fontsize={\scriptsize},tabsize=4"
inline true
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
&
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset listings
lstparams "language=Python,fontfamily=tt,fontsize={\scriptsize},tabsize=4"
inline true
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
|
\end_layout

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset listings
lstparams "language=Python,fontfamily=tt,fontsize={\scriptsize},tabsize=4"
inline true
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
~
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 a typographic difference and not a mathematical one.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Two entries repay a second look.
 The reflected forms —
 
\begin_inset listings
lstparams "language=Python,fontfamily=tt,fontsize={\scriptsize},tabsize=4"
inline true
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
__rmul__
\end_layout

\end_inset

 and its siblings —
 exist because a binary operation must be dispatched on one of its operands,
 and the left one may know nothing of the right;
 they are what allows a scalar action 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda\cdot v$
\end_inset

 to be written with the scalar first while the vector supplies the definition.
 And mathematics overloads 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\cdot\right|$
\end_inset

 for both a projection onto a magnitude and the cardinality of a set,
 where Python separates them into 
\begin_inset listings
lstparams "language=Python,fontfamily=tt,fontsize={\scriptsize},tabsize=4"
inline true
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
abs
\end_layout

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset listings
lstparams "language=Python,fontfamily=tt,fontsize={\scriptsize},tabsize=4"
inline true
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
len
\end_layout

\end_inset

 —
 a distinction worth keeping,
 since the two coincide for no carrier used here.
\end_layout

