\begin_layout Subsection
Structures
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
An algebraic structure is a carrier set together with operations on it,
 constants it distinguishes,
 and laws they satisfy;
 a type is a carrier together with the methods it implements.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "tab:structures"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 lists the standard structures against the operations that generate them.
 It names no methods,
 deliberately:
 the spelling of 
\begin_inset Formula $\wedge$
\end_inset

 or of 
\begin_inset Formula $\leq$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "tab:operators"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

's business,
 and composing the two tables gives the declaration a carrier has to make.
\end_layout

