\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "tab:structures"

\end_inset

 Standard structures and their generators,
 by group:
 the multiplicative tower,
 the order tower,
 and the lattice tower.
 Each group is cumulative downwards,
 a monoid carrying whatever a magma does.
 The right-hand column is a generating set and not an inventory:
 a Boolean algebra offers 
\begin_inset Formula $\vee$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\setminus$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbin{\triangle}$
\end_inset

 as well,
 all of them definable from the four listed.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The middle column of that table is the one a type cannot express.
 A type can declare that an operation exists,
 and what it consumes and returns;
 it cannot declare that the operation is associative,
 that complementation is an involution,
 or that meet distributes over join.
 Signatures are checked mechanically,
 laws are not.
 Two consequences follow,
 and both organise what comes later.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The first is that the laws have to be discharged elsewhere —
 by proof,
 and by tests standing in for proof.
 A structure presented as a type is therefore a contract:
 a small set of operations that a carrier must supply,
 together with a body of laws it is asserted to satisfy and against which it is exercised.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The second is more productive.
 Once the laws are granted they may be used,
 because a law relating two operations is a definition of either one in terms of the other.
 De Morgan's laws 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
a\vee b=\lnot(\lnot a\wedge\lnot b),\qquad a\wedge b=\lnot(\lnot a\vee\lnot b)
\]

\end_inset

are theorems in a Boolean algebra;
 read as code they are a pair of default implementations,
 and a carrier supplying 
\begin_inset Formula $\lnot$
\end_inset

 together with either one of 
\begin_inset Formula $\vee$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\wedge$
\end_inset

 obtains the other at no cost.
 That is the whole content of the last row of 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "tab:structures"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

.
 A partial order needs only 
\begin_inset Formula $\leq$
\end_inset

,
 since 
\begin_inset Formula $a\geq b$
\end_inset

 is the reflection 
\begin_inset Formula $b\leq a$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $=$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\neq$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $<$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $>$
\end_inset

 follow from the two.
 A bounded structure needs only its two constants for finite joins and meets to be defined by folding,
 with the bounds serving as the empty cases of 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "tab:constants"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
An abstract type in this reading holds the generators of a structure as its abstract methods and the theorems as its concrete ones.
 Defining a new carrier means choosing a generating set of operations and supplying exactly those;
 the rest is inherited,
 and inherited correctly,
 because the inherited definitions are the laws themselves.
 Where a family of derivations closes a cycle —
 
\begin_inset Formula $\vee$
\end_inset

 from 
\begin_inset Formula $\wedge$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\wedge$
\end_inset

 from 
\begin_inset Formula $\vee$
\end_inset

 —
 any one member may be supplied and the others follow,
 which leaves the choice of generators with the carrier instead of fixing it in the structure.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Indicators,
 and what generalises
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One entry of 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "tab:objects"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 deserves separate mention,
 since what follows is a generalisation of it and of nothing else.
 A subset 
\begin_inset Formula $A\subseteq I$
\end_inset

 is determined by its indicator function 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\chi_{A}\colon I\to\mathbb{B},\qquad\chi_{A}(i)=\top\iff i\in A
\]

\end_inset

so that 
\begin_inset listings
lstparams "language=Python,fontfamily=tt,fontsize={\scriptsize},tabsize=4"
inline true
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
frozenset[I]
\end_layout

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset listings
lstparams "language=Python,fontfamily=tt,fontsize={\scriptsize},tabsize=4"
inline true
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
dict[I,
 bool]
\end_layout

\end_inset

 carry the same information:
 membership is a Boolean value,
 and the container is a map into a Boolean algebra.
 The set operations transport across the identification pointwise —
 union to 
\begin_inset Formula $\vee$
\end_inset

,
 intersection to 
\begin_inset Formula $\wedge$
\end_inset

,
 complement to 
\begin_inset Formula $\lnot$
\end_inset

 —
 so the two agree not merely as data but as algebras,
 and the two set rows of 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "tab:constants"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 are its first two rows read through 
\begin_inset Formula $\chi$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Written that way,
 nothing requires the codomain to be 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{B}$
\end_inset

.
 Replace it by any carrier 
\begin_inset Formula $W$
\end_inset

 meeting the same contract and the same pointwise definitions produce a graded set,
 one in which membership is a matter of degree;
 take 
\begin_inset Formula $W=\mathbb{B}$
\end_inset

 and the ordinary notion is recovered exactly,
 which is the sense in which the generalisation is conservative.
 Allow 
\begin_inset Formula $W$
\end_inset

 to be the type of the container itself and the construction closes under nesting:
 a map 
\begin_inset Formula $I\to(I\to W)$
\end_inset

 is a weighted adjacency matrix,
 its rows are neighbourhoods,
 and one further level is a hypergraph.
 The sections that follow build the carrier 
\begin_inset Formula $W$
\end_inset

 first and the containers on top of it.
\end_layout

